# Line endings. This file sat unresolved with conflict markers for most of the
# project, and git parsed both halves, so the rules below were half in force
# and the working tree drifted: agent_data/*.py were CRLF on disk while
# allocate.py arrived in the container as LF. An agent then spent commands on
# `xxd`, `od -c` and byte-level rewriting to get a clean diff -- paid tokens
# spent on the repo's state rather than on the task.
#
# Resolved as the union of both sides. Neither half was wrong; they were
# written for different reasons and both reasons still hold.

# From the first side: shell scripts run under dash inside the image. With
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows a checkout rewrites them to CRLF and the build
# dies on `set: Illegal option -`, which is how the first build of this
# scaffold failed.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# From the second side: same failure, different message. A Dockerfile converted
# to CRLF fails with "/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter", a confusing error a long way
# from its cause.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf

# Staged into the sandbox and read by the agent. Keep them byte-stable so the
# fixture is identical across platforms and the history stays reproducible.
*.py            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf

# A notebook is JSON, but the extension does not say so, and a checkout that
# rewrites it changes every line of the diff whenever a cell is run.
*.ipynb         text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

*.log           text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
# Same reason as .cmd: these are launched by Windows shells, and a PowerShell
# script checked out with LF still runs, but keeping the pair consistent stops
# the working tree churning every time one is edited on either platform.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Eval logs are zip archives, not text. Without this a checkout corrupts every
# committed run.
*.eval          binary
